  <p>Supplements are the last step, not the first. Strength coach Jordan Hoppel's order of operations: handle the basics, then consider what has the most research behind it.</p>

  <JordanQuote>Handle the basics before supplements, and get health advice from your doctor, someone measuring the levels in your body.</JordanQuote>

  <h2>Whey protein</h2>
  <p>Another complete protein source and an easy way to get protein in when it's hard through the day. People shouldn't be reliant on shakes. Food comes first; see <a href="/how-to-eat-for-muscle-protein-and-calories/">how to eat for muscle</a>.</p>

  <h2>Creatine</h2>
  <p>Very highly studied, and Jordan says people who want to take it should. Start around 5 g a day. Full details are in the <a href="/creatine-guide-for-lifters/">creatine guide</a>.</p>

  <Media type="image" alt="Whey protein and creatine tubs on a gym shelf" caption="The two Jordan names." brief="Photo of the whey and creatine Jordan uses (only if he is comfortable naming brands)." />

  <h2>Caffeine and pre-workout</h2>
  <p>If you want to take it and you feel good when you take it, take it. But if it affects your sleep, don't take it for the workout; that's a poor trade-off. Make sure any caffeine or pre-workout isn't affecting your sleep. See <a href="/sleep-for-lifters/">sleep for lifters</a>.</p>

  <h2>Sleep supplements</h2>
  <p>For people who sleep badly, magnesium, melatonin, and GABA can also help, according to Jordan.</p>
